Events.  Good for him.  My statements stand.  Wouldn't allocate unless the fund has uncorrelated alpha AND has the negative beta tail handled. But couldn't figure out whether any fund has both particularly with a short track record and the rarity of tails

@0xHenri_ I think alpha can not be proven with low frequency trading strategies.  High frequency perhaps.  As to tail risk funds it's even harder given sample size. 

As to a general framework of finding alpha in managers I place low weight on performance and high weight on process

Yes I follow and respect your work.  I think what you wrote in the article shows that you may have a fair to large amount of alpha.  However tail risk hedging without alpha is simply negative beta.  It's impossible that reducing one's tail risk via convexity buying is positive beta   Do you agree with the last sentence?

And I think diversification is cheaper.  As an options guy from the beginning of my career I really wish convexity was alpha.  I've learned over the four decades of trading options that convexity is mostly always expensive relative to its value and thus not useful unless you can time it which most can't but some can

@Richard_Casey Oh boy the hidden trades which cover the carry that blow up when a tail event occurs is by far my greatest criticism of tail risk funds.

@ConvexityDredge Fair enough.  I'm sorry I can't get by the Upton Sinclair quote.  I've never been successful having an unbiased open minded discussion with tail risk hedging salespeople.  My problem not yours and as I said I think you probably have alpha.  But that is literally NOT the point

